**定理 80.1（rounded head and whole tail）。** Installation really constructs T=2^(h+2)(K+4), then a Fibonacci list until F_(J-2)>=2T+2 with J>=6. All j>=J have R>=(F_(j-2)-2)/2>=T. Since U0 V0<=1-D/2, U0^R V0^S<=exp(-RD/2)<=exp(-2(K+4))<=2^-(K+4). Thus returning zero controls the ENTIRE true tail by epsilon/16. The retained integer h alone does not pay for constructing T; that O(h+log(K+4)) bit integer and the Fibonacci list are installation work.

For the finite head, w=h+p+bit_length(K+8)+16. Reconstructed bases are EXACT integers on the 2^-w grid (w>=h+p). Every multiplication on [0,1] is rounded down by less than 2^-w, including binary repeated squaring. Induction on multiplication degree proves the rounded power of degree n has error at most max(n-1,0)*2^-w: squaring doubles the earlier error and adds one rounding unit; multiplication adds both earlier errors and one unit. A rounded product of the R and S powers has error at most (R+S+1)*2^-w. At the stopping index F_(J-2)<4T+4 by minimality, so for j<J, R+S+1<8T+16<2^(h+bit_length(K+8)+8). Thus scalar arithmetic error <2^(-p-8). Stored anchors have norm <=2 on a valid acquisition. Final coordinate rounding has Euclidean error <eta. Including loss perturbation, anchor perturbation and numerical rounding, head error is at most epsilon/256+12eta+2*2^(-p-8)+eta<epsilon/16<E. The generous constants also cover j=3,4,5, zero bases and exponent zero. Universal estimates, not samples, justify every query.

The whole query is the finite frame Q<binary digits>; with EOF after the semicolon. Every digit is read. Empty, truncated, nonbinary, negative, leading-zero multi-digit, j<3, extra-semicolon and trailing-token strings return finite ERR. The parser stores a value saturated at J and a residue mod 3, plus finite flags, rather than an unbounded j. Once saturated it still reads all later digits. On a malformed word it also consumes the entire query before ERR. Gamma word parsing rejects truncation, nonbinary codes and trailing fields. Outputs have an explicit finite V frame (three signed dyadic numerators plus precision) or R frame (three finite rational numerator/denominator pairs), or ERR. Installation may be retained once for repeated queries; an uncached evaluation pays installation again.

